  "result": "I opened the issue: https://github.com/acme/textstats/issues/13\n\nI ran it first and confirmed the bug: `word_count(\"\")` returns `1`. The cause is in `textstats/__init__.py:6`: the function uses `text.split(\" \")`, and `\"\".split(\" \")` returns `['']`, a list with one empty string, so it counts 1.\n\nThe issue also notes that the same code miscounts repeated spaces, spaces at the start or end, tabs and newlines. For example, `word_count(\"a  b\")` returns 3. It suggests switching to `text.split()`, which `average_word_length` already uses, and adding a test for the empty string. I didn't change any code.",
